00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:58,080
Now I've switched years ago to making suggestions on a personal basis to our patients.

00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:06,440
So if a person is doing chemo from a standard standpoint, so they're doing chemo, should they detox

00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:07,440
them?

00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:11,760
Should they do things to detoxify the chemo itself?

00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:19,200
Well, there's a theory, which I adhere to, that chemo really does the majority of its work

00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:25,000
within the first 48 hours of administration and after that it could just be toxic to our bodies.

00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,200
So after that, we do want to support detoxification pathways.

00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,400
Now I already have everybody on some things.

00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:38,040
Should we add some things to help with that?

00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:39,800
Look at what I add.

00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:46,440
So when I make a recommendation based upon a cheek swab test, we want to stick to that until

00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,400
we get some of the other data back.

00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:55,960
And we get your genetics back that shows that certain pathways need more support than

00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:57,760
we would add that.

00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:02,600
Or if a person started chemo before we got their genetics back, and let's say they just

00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:07,600
did really poorly with the chemo because we've had that before, because that's an indication.

00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:14,640
So if a person does chemo, let's say they had chemo on Monday, and then by Wednesday they

00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:22,240
were throwing up sick, and they had to take anti-naudia medicine, they were in bed, it just

00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:25,240
literally wiped them out for three days.

00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:26,240
What does that tell us?

00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:31,800
Well, there's a tell us point that chemo was too strong, they need to dial that back a little

00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:32,800
bit.

00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,880
Maybe that's a conversation that one would need to have with their oncologist.

00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:40,560
Does it tell us that the chemo was working?

00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:47,080
Well, well, not really, it tells us that you had adverse effects of that chemo.

00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:54,280
What it mostly tells us is that you don't detoxify that chemo therapy very well.

00:02:54,280 --> 00:03:02,440
So if a person has severe adverse reactions to a chemo therapy, that gives us an indication

00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:08,840
that you don't detoxify that chemo therapy very well.

00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:14,520
Chemo therapy's detoxified partially on the pod pathway, different cytokine, cytokine,

00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:20,160
P450 pathways, and different net pathways, MAT pathways.

00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:25,640
So if you're reacting, people that react poorly to it, they probably have a lot of defects

00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:26,800
on those pathways.

00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,960
They're just slower detoxifiers of things that have to go through that pathway.

00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:35,560
There are some people, and some people that might be listening to this call, that they did

00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:40,120
six rounds of chemo and had almost no ill effects, maybe a little bit of nausea, but

00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:43,000
they just kind of soared right through that.

00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,800
I just talked to a gentleman today that did chemo for his prostate cancer, had literally

00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:56,880
no ill effects, and he's doing great and no ill effects.

00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:57,880
What's that tell us?

00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:03,920
Well, that tells us that he has really good detoxification pathways from a genetic standpoint.

00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:09,520
And sure enough, we did his genes, and he does have really good detoxification pathways.

00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:16,400
So your ability to get rid of things has to do with not just your genes, but the genes

00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,160
do play a big role of that.

00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:25,040
So if you have defects on your detoxification pathways, you're going to be a slower detoxifier.

00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:30,240
It also has to do with things beyond genetics, meaning how healthy your liver was going

00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:32,520
into this whole situation.

00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:40,720
If you had exbed exposed to different toxins, alcohol, other environmental toxins throughout

00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:46,840
your life, that damaged your liver, then you're going to be a slower detoxifier, even if

00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:50,160
you don't have any genetic defects on your detoxification pathway.

00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:58,040
So there's different factors, genetic and epigenetic factors that can slow a person's rate of detoxification

00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:04,280
and if your rate of detoxification is hindered, then you're going to have more ill effects

00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:07,480
from any toxin that you're exposed to.

00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:15,960
So an individual basis, then, should I add supplements, other nutrition, help me detox if

00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:22,600
I don't know what my genetic pathways are, well, then I would go, how are you doing symptom-wise

00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:23,600
post chemotherapy?

00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,600
I'm doing pretty good.

00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:27,600
Well, then I wouldn't change anything.

00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,240
I'm doing it horrible, okay, then we did ever talk.

00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:32,040
So that's how I would make that decision.

